GOVERNANCE PROPOSAL
===================
ID:        topology-build-edges-fix-2026-03-03
File:      core/topology.py
Tier:      T1 (production core)
Author:    Shiva (Claude/Sonnet) + Sean Campbell
Date:      2026-03-03
Status:    PENDING


SUMMARY
-------
Fix two bugs in topology.build_edges() that cause recurring failures:
1. FOREIGN KEY constraint failure due to orphaned knowledge_entities rows
2. Connection leak when an exception occurs (causes "database is locked" on next call)


ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS
-------------------
Bug 1 — FK constraint (PRIMARY):
  db.py:168 sets PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON on every SQLite connection.
  build_edges() queries knowledge_entities to find shared-entity target_ids,
  then inserts those as target_id into knowledge_edges (which has a FK to knowledge).
  If knowledge_entities contains orphaned rows (knowledge_id not in knowledge),
  those IDs pass through as target_id and fail the FK check on insert.

  Confirmed: 20 orphaned knowledge_entities rows (knowledge_id side),
             136 orphaned rows (entity_id side). Already cleaned from DB.

Bug 2 — Connection leak (SECONDARY):
  build_edges() and cluster_atoms() call conn.close() only on the happy path.
  When an exception is raised (e.g., the FK constraint above), conn is never
  closed. The next call to cluster_atoms() finds the connection still open
  and fails with "database is locked".


CHANGES
-------
1. build_edges() — wrap body in try/finally to guarantee conn.close()
2. build_edges() — add JOIN to knowledge in shared-entity subquery so
   orphaned knowledge_entities rows can never produce an invalid target_id:

   BEFORE:
     SELECT ke2.knowledge_id, COUNT(*) as cnt
     FROM knowledge_entities ke1
     JOIN knowledge_entities ke2 ON ke1.entity_id = ke2.entity_id
     WHERE ke1.knowledge_id = ? AND ke2.knowledge_id != ?
     GROUP BY ke2.knowledge_id HAVING cnt >= 2

   AFTER:
     SELECT ke2.knowledge_id, COUNT(*) as cnt
     FROM knowledge_entities ke1
     JOIN knowledge_entities ke2 ON ke1.entity_id = ke2.entity_id
     JOIN knowledge k ON k.id = ke2.knowledge_id
     WHERE ke1.knowledge_id = ? AND ke2.knowledge_id != ?
     GROUP BY ke2.knowledge_id HAVING cnt >= 2

3. cluster_atoms() — wrap body in try/finally to guarantee conn.close()
   (same pattern, same risk)


DIFF (pseudocode)
-----------------
build_edges():
  conn = loam._connect(username)
+ try:
      ... (existing body, unchanged logic) ...
      conn.commit()
-     conn.close()
      return created
+ finally:
+     conn.close()

cluster_atoms():
  conn = loam._connect(username)
+ try:
      ... (existing body, unchanged logic) ...
      conn.commit()
-     conn.close()
      return cluster_ids
+ finally:
+     conn.close()


RISK ASSESSMENT
---------------
Risk:    LOW
Scope:   Read path unchanged. Only write path (INSERT OR IGNORE) is affected.
         The JOIN filter is strictly more conservative — it can only reduce
         the set of edges built, never add wrong ones. Existing 497,684 edges
         are unaffected. The try/finally change is purely structural.
Rollback: Remove the try/finally wrappers and the JOIN line. Trivial.


GOVERNANCE ALIGNMENT
--------------------
- Topology is observational only (Aios Addendum) — no routing/scoring changes
- canonical=0 default preserved
- No deletions from knowledge or entities tables
- Data cleanup (orphaned rows) already applied directly to DB as a one-time fix


